<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="skill">tools</ansi>

Working the wild takes the right tool. Skinning a carcass, butchering it,
felling a tree or sawing a log all depend mostly on your body and your tool,
and only a little on practice.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Tool Types ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">knife</ansi>          skinning and cutting meat
  <ansi fg="yellow">cleaver</ansi>        butchering through joints for bone and fat
  <ansi fg="yellow">bone saw</ansi>       horn, antler and tusk
  <ansi fg="yellow">axe</ansi>            felling trees
  <ansi fg="yellow">saw</ansi>            ripping logs into planks, staves and shafts
  <ansi fg="yellow">scraper</ansi>        fleshing hides before curing
  <ansi fg="yellow">sickle</ansi>         herbs, reeds and fibre
  <ansi fg="yellow">carving knife</ansi>  bone, horn and fine woodwork

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Tool Quality ━━━</ansi>

A better tool works better and can produce better material. The tier caps
the best grade you can bring home:

  <ansi fg="yellow">crude</ansi>        up to standard   (a weapon pressed into service)
  <ansi fg="yellow">iron</ansi>         up to fine
  <ansi fg="yellow">steel</ansi>        up to superb
  <ansi fg="yellow">masterwork</ansi>   up to pristine

A one-handed dagger or sword will do as a crude knife, and a hatchet as a
crude axe or cleaver, but a real tool is far better. Smiths forge them at a
forge. You only need to carry the tool; the best one you have is used.

